## Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA)

## Structure

```typescript
test('adds two numbers', () => {
  // ARRANGE — set up
  const calculator = new Calculator();

  // ACT — invoke the behaviour
  const result = calculator.add(2, 3);

  // ASSERT — verify the outcome
  expect(result).toBe(5);
});
```

## Benefits

- **Readability**: immediately clear what's being tested
- **Single responsibility**: one Act per test = one thing being tested
- **Debugging**: when a test fails, the three sections tell you exactly where to look

## React Component Example

```typescript
import { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react';
import userEvent from '@testing-library/user-event';
import Counter from './Counter';

test('increments count when button is clicked', async () => {
  // ARRANGE
  const user = userEvent.setup();
  render(<Counter initialCount={0} />);

  // ACT
  await user.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /increment/i }));

  // ASSERT
  expect(screen.getByText('Count: 1')).toBeInTheDocument();
});
```

## Async Example

```typescript
test('fetches and displays user name', async () => {
  // ARRANGE
  jest.spyOn(api, 'getUser').mockResolvedValue({ name: 'Aaron' });
  render(<UserProfile userId="1" />);

  // ACT (implicit — fetch triggered on mount)

  // ASSERT
  expect(await screen.findByText('Aaron')).toBeInTheDocument();
});
```

## Variation: Given-When-Then

BDD style — same concept, different names:
- **Given** (Arrange)
- **When** (Act)
- **Then** (Assert)

